course: dictionary
lesson: 8017
title: A vote on one big question
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A referendum is a vote in which everyone in a country or area decides one important question directly. Read the clues: «hold a referendum» and «vote in a referendum» show it is something people do together, and the question is usually answered yes or no.
explain: Look at the example: «In a referendum everyone votes yes or no on one question, such as changing Ireland's constitution.» The clues are one question and yes or no. Citizens decide the question themselves, instead of choosing people to decide for them.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «In a vote everyone decides one question themselves.» If that still makes sense, you have understood it. A referendum is special because it is one big question, not many choices.
fact: A referendum is a vote where all the voters of a country or area decide one important question directly.
fact: «Referendum» is a noun, and its plural can be referendums or referenda.
fact: In 2016 the UK held a referendum on leaving the European Union, and the UK left in January 2020.
fact: In 1999 Australians voted in a referendum on becoming a republic, and most voted no.
fact: In 2014 most Scottish voters chose to stay in a referendum on Scottish independence.
exercise: «In a referendum everyone votes yes or no on one question.» What is a referendum? | *A vote where all the voters decide one important question | A choice of people to rule the country for five years | A quiz with many questions | A race between two teams
exercise: «In 1999 Australians voted in a referendum on becoming a republic.» What is a republic? | A country with two capital cities | A country ruled by one king | A small island country | *A country without a king or queen
exercise: «The UK left the European Union in 2020 after a referendum in 2016.» In which year was the referendum? | 2020 | 1999 | *2016 | 2014
test: «Most Scottish voters chose to stay in a 2014 referendum.» What did most Scottish voters choose? | To become independent from the UK | *To stay in the UK | To change the country's constitution | To leave the European Union
test: Why is a referendum different from an ordinary vote? | It is always won by children | It is a vote that picks a king | It is held only once every hundred years | *It decides one big question, and the voters decide it themselves
exam: «Results can be close and split families and friends, so arguing politely matters.» What should people do when a referendum result is close? | *Argue politely and respect other views | Hide from the news | Stop talking to friends who voted differently | Shout at everyone who disagrees
